  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Near-normal temperatures with the weather pattern during this \n  week, along with mostly dry conditions.\n\n- Increasing winds Tuesday, especially across northwest Montana and\n  the higher terrain. \n\n- Scattered rain showers Tuesday night into early Wednesday then \n  slight warming trend into the remainder of the week.\n\n- Confidence increasing for a warmer and drier than normal early \n  October beyond the 7 day forecast. \n\nValley inversions in September today started with weak valley\ninversions which may keep realization of peak warming subdued \nand hold off afternoon breezes until late afternoon.\n\nTuesday as a weak wave moves through the region, breezy \nconditions are expected to develop. Strongest winds will be north\nof I-90 and in the mountains but valleys of northwest MT could \neasily see gusts into the 20s on Tuesday. Due to winds developing \nearly lake recreationalists will be less likely to be surprised \nduring the afternoon. That said, prepare for choppy conditions on\narea lakes.\n\nOvernight into Wednesday a few showers develop, mainly along the\nIdaho/ Montana border and in Glacier National Park. These showers\nwill be light in nature and the probability of a wetting rain, or\ngreater than 1/10 of an inch, is 40 to 60% in the highest \nterrain. Most valleys, with the elevated winds even overnight, \nwill see increased effects of downsloping. \n\nLonger term the models have been very consistent is demonstrating\na building ridge of high pressure. This outcome is common in the\nfall but temperatures in the National Blend of Models are forecast\nto be 10 to 15 degrees above normal. The reason this discussion\nbegan with 'valley inversions' is because, depending if valley\ninversions develop and their strength, high temperature forecasts\nmay be difficult for the start to October. Current models predict\nfor the ridge axis to be west of the Rockies, which would assist \nin the development of valley inversions. Additionally, Pacific \ntropical activity could throw a wrench into longer term forecasts,\ncausing more variation in the pattern or exciting ridge breakdowns,\nmore to come.",
